1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,449
Aarav's phone fits in his pocket.

2
00:00:02,449 --> 00:00:06,609
Yet it can do far more than a computer
that once filled a whole room.

3
00:00:06,959 --> 00:00:08,153
What changed?

4
00:00:08,153 --> 00:00:09,218
Electronics.

5
00:00:09,718 --> 00:00:14,341
In nineteen forty six, a computer
called ENIAC used seventeen thousand,

6
00:00:14,341 --> 00:00:17,212
four hundred and sixty eight vacuum tubes.

7
00:00:17,562 --> 00:00:19,719
It weighed about twenty seven tonnes.

8
00:00:20,069 --> 00:00:24,951
Today, a chip smaller than your fingernail
holds billions of tiny switches.

9
00:00:25,601 --> 00:00:27,241
So what is electronics?

10
00:00:27,591 --> 00:00:31,954
Electronics is the branch of science
and engineering that controls the

11
00:00:31,954 --> 00:00:33,333
flow of electrons.

12
00:00:33,683 --> 00:00:38,003
Those electrons can move through a
vacuum, through a gas, or through a

13
00:00:38,003 --> 00:00:39,701
semiconductor like silicon.

14
00:00:40,051 --> 00:00:41,914
The key word is control.

15
00:00:41,914 --> 00:00:44,799
Electronics does not just use electricity.

16
00:00:44,799 --> 00:00:45,948
It steers it.

17
00:00:46,598 --> 00:00:48,697
Let's see the difference on a breadboard.

18
00:00:49,047 --> 00:00:51,333
Here is a simple electrical circuit.

19
00:00:51,333 --> 00:00:53,533
A battery, a switch and a bulb.

20
00:00:53,883 --> 00:00:56,267
Close the switch, and electrons flow.

21
00:00:56,267 --> 00:00:57,608
The bulb lights up.

22
00:00:57,958 --> 00:00:59,793
Open it, and the flow stops.

23
00:00:59,793 --> 00:01:02,124
There are only two states, on or off.

24
00:01:02,774 --> 00:01:04,617
Now an electronic circuit.

25
00:01:04,617 --> 00:01:08,325
A tiny signal from a microphone
goes into a transistor.

26
00:01:08,675 --> 00:01:13,362
The transistor uses that tiny signal to
control a much bigger current through

27
00:01:13,362 --> 00:01:14,333
the speaker.

28
00:01:14,683 --> 00:01:15,854
Watch the scope.

29
00:01:15,854 --> 00:01:17,520
The input wave is small.

30
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:21,936
The output wave has the same shape,
but it is a hundred times bigger.

31
00:01:22,286 --> 00:01:24,338
This is called amplification.

32
00:01:24,338 --> 00:01:26,844
A small signal controls a big one.

33
00:01:26,844 --> 00:01:28,357
That is electronics.

34
00:01:29,007 --> 00:01:31,165
Electrical circuits use energy.

35
00:01:31,165 --> 00:01:33,323
Electronic circuits control it.

36
00:01:34,123 --> 00:01:36,409
Now, how many electrons are moving?

37
00:01:36,409 --> 00:01:37,655
Let's work it out.

38
00:01:38,005 --> 00:01:41,912
Current is the charge that
passes a point every second.

39
00:01:41,912 --> 00:01:44,104
So I equals Q divided by t.

40
00:01:44,804 --> 00:01:47,042
Charge comes in tiny packets.

41
00:01:47,042 --> 00:01:52,067
Each electron carries a charge e,
one point six times ten to the minus

42
00:01:52,067 --> 00:01:53,489
nineteen coulombs.

43
00:01:54,189 --> 00:01:58,175
So n electrons carry a
charge Q equals n times e.

44
00:01:58,875 --> 00:02:02,993
Put the two together, and n
equals I times t, divided by e.

45
00:02:03,693 --> 00:02:07,313
Current in amperes, time in
seconds, charge in coulombs.

46
00:02:07,313 --> 00:02:10,236
And n is just a count, so it has no unit.

47
00:02:10,886 --> 00:02:12,045
Worked example.

48
00:02:12,045 --> 00:02:15,501
A phone charger sends two
amperes into the battery.

49
00:02:15,851 --> 00:02:19,070
How many electrons pass through
the cable in one second?

50
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:24,498
n equals two times one, divided by
one point six times ten to the minus

51
00:02:24,498 --> 00:02:25,398
nineteen.

52
00:02:25,938 --> 00:02:30,777
That is one point two five times ten
to the nineteen electrons, every single

53
00:02:30,777 --> 00:02:31,677
second.

54
00:02:32,094 --> 00:02:35,491
So how did we learn to
control so many electrons?

55
00:02:35,491 --> 00:02:36,602
Step by step.

56
00:02:36,952 --> 00:02:41,561
In nineteen oh four, John Ambrose
Fleming made the vacuum tube diode.

57
00:02:41,561 --> 00:02:43,926
It let current flow only one way.

58
00:02:44,276 --> 00:02:47,390
In nineteen oh six, Lee
de Forest added a grid.

59
00:02:47,390 --> 00:02:50,202
His triode could make
a weak signal stronger.

60
00:02:50,552 --> 00:02:54,241
But tubes were big, they got hot,
and they used a lot of power.

61
00:02:54,591 --> 00:02:58,921
In nineteen forty seven, Bardeen,
Brattain and Shockley built the first

62
00:02:58,921 --> 00:03:01,095
transistor, from a semiconductor.

63
00:03:01,445 --> 00:03:06,033
In nineteen fifty eight, Jack Kilby
put several parts on one small chip.

64
00:03:06,033 --> 00:03:08,118
The integrated circuit was born.

65
00:03:08,468 --> 00:03:13,558
In nineteen seventy one came the first
microprocessor, the brain of a computer

66
00:03:13,558 --> 00:03:14,970
on a single chip.

67
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:19,416
Later, microcontrollers put a
whole small computer on one chip.

68
00:03:19,416 --> 00:03:22,843
Each step made circuits
smaller, faster and cheaper.

69
00:03:23,493 --> 00:03:25,935
And the scope of
electronics keeps growing.

70
00:03:26,285 --> 00:03:28,917
Communication, like
phones and the internet.

71
00:03:28,917 --> 00:03:31,359
Entertainment, like television and music.

72
00:03:31,709 --> 00:03:33,383
Defence, like radar.

73
00:03:33,383 --> 00:03:36,620
Industry, like robots
and automatic machines.

74
00:03:36,970 --> 00:03:39,826
And medicine, like heart
monitors and scanners.

75
00:03:39,826 --> 00:03:42,654
We will explore each one
later in this series.

76
00:03:43,304 --> 00:03:44,769
Now, the exam trap.

77
00:03:45,119 --> 00:03:48,854
Many students think electrons
flow the same way as current.

78
00:03:48,854 --> 00:03:49,866
They do not.

79
00:03:50,216 --> 00:03:54,134
Conventional current flows from
plus to minus outside the cell.

80
00:03:54,134 --> 00:03:57,454
Electrons drift the other
way, from minus to plus.

81
00:03:58,104 --> 00:03:59,791
Where is this asked?

82
00:03:59,791 --> 00:04:02,815
In PUC board papers, as short answers.

83
00:04:02,815 --> 00:04:07,809
Define electronics, name an invention
and its year, or count electrons

84
00:04:07,809 --> 00:04:09,870
with n equals I t by e.

85
00:04:10,520 --> 00:04:11,420
Try this.

86
00:04:11,312 --> 00:04:15,588
A current of three point two milliamperes
flows through a small lamp for

87
00:04:15,588 --> 00:04:16,573
five seconds.

88
00:04:16,573 --> 00:04:18,851
How many electrons pass through it?

89
00:04:24,251 --> 00:04:25,151
B.

90
00:04:24,698 --> 00:04:27,051
One times ten to the seventeen.

91
00:04:27,401 --> 00:04:29,952
First, change milliamperes to amperes.

92
00:04:29,952 --> 00:04:33,975
That is three point two times
ten to the minus three amperes.

93
00:04:34,325 --> 00:04:36,453
Charge is current times time.

94
00:04:36,453 --> 00:04:39,570
That gives zero point
zero one six coulombs.

95
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:44,623
Divide by one point six times ten to
the minus nineteen, and you get one

96
00:04:44,623 --> 00:04:46,545
times ten to the seventeen.

97
00:04:47,195 --> 00:04:48,716
Screenshot this card.

98
00:04:51,916 --> 00:04:55,046
Next, vacuum tubes and
semiconductor devices.

99
00:04:55,046 --> 00:04:58,799
Why did the tiny transistor
beat the big glowing tube?
